  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon an upper-level trough, associated with a Pacific Cold \nFront, with strong southerly flow approaches Montana. Due to a \nstrong surface pressure gradient and strong low-level and mid-level \nwinds it will be windy this afternoon across North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. The Pacific Cold Front will move through \nthe area this afternoon/evening. This will bring strong winds to the \nRocky Mountain Front and plains west of I-15 this evening though \nearly Saturday morning. As the cold front moves through North-\ncentral Montana, rain showers have the potential to bring gusty to \nstrong winds. Tonight light snow will affect the Rocky Mountain \nFront. See the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios Section for \ndetails. \n\nOn Saturday the upper-level trough remains over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This combined with the cold front \nwill bring cold temperatures (slightly below seasonal averages) to \nthe area. Saturday morning snow will linger along the Rocky Mountain \nFront and it will be mostly dry elsewhere. On Sunday an upper-level \nridge begins to build in over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will keep cool temperatures over the area \nwith mostly dry weather. On Monday a weak upper-level trough moves \nover the area. This will allow temperatures to warm up some with \nisolated showers around. On Tuesday an upper-level ridge briefly \nbuilds in over the area. This will allow warm temperatures to remain \nwith dry weather. Tuesday night into Wednesday the upper-level ridge \nbreaks down. This will bring strong winds to North-central Montana. \nOn Wednesday and Thursday there will be zonal flow over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring breezy, \nwarm, and dry conditions to the area on Wednesday and Thursday. For \ndetails for Friday through next weekend see the Forecast Confidence \nand Scenarios section. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAlong the Rocky Mountain and plains west of I-15 there is greater \nthan an 80% chance for wind gusts of 55 mph or greater this evening \nthrough tomorrow morning. Since hi-res models suggested that \nstrong winds could last until noon tomorrow, the High Wind Warning\nfor those areas was extended until noon tomorrow. In the Sweet \nGrass Hills there is greater than an 80% chance for wind gusts of \n55 mph or greater from this evening through early tomorrow \nmorning. However since this is a localized area a High Wind \nWarning was not issued for this area. The HREF (Hi-res ensemble \nforecast) showed strong 850 mb winds greater than 40 kts over the \nbears paw mountains associated with the cold front. As a result a \nHigh Wind Warning was issued for the Bears Paw Mountains from 8 PM\ntonight through 6 AM tomorrow morning. \n\nTonight across the golden triangle region associated with the cold \nfront a line of showers will move through. At least isolated \nportions of the line have at least a low chance to produce wind \ngusts in excess of 58 mph (severe wind gusts). Tonight along the \nnorthern Rocky Mountain Front there is at least a 80% chance for \ngreater than a tenth of an inch of snow. Tonight along the higher \npeaks of the northern Rocky Mountain Front (the eastern portion of\nGlacier National Park) there is a greater than a 60% chance for \nan inch of snow.\n\nNext Friday through next Sunday some of the models and ensembles are \npredicting a snowfall event. Two of the four clusters which \nrepresents around 50% of the ensemble members have an upper-level \ntrough over Montana next Friday through next Sunday. This \nscenario would bring well-below seasonal averages for temperatures\nwith the potential for both lower-elevation and mountain snow \nnext Friday through next Sunday. These two clusters differ on how\nstrong and how far east the upper-level trough is. A stronger \ntrough centered over Montana would bring more snow and colder \ntemperatures than a trough that is less strong and/or further \neast. The other two clusters which represents the other 50% of \nensemble membership have a upper-level ridge over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana next Friday through next \nSunday. This will bring above seasonal average temperatures with \ndry weather to the area next Friday through next Sunday. -IG",
